        nameJa: "じかんの いいかた",
        meaning: "telling the time, and 'at' a time",
        explanation: "Say the hour with 〜じ and the minutes with 〜ふん (it voices to 〜ぷん after some numbers). To say you do something AT that time, add に after it: しちじに おきます, 'I get up at seven'. はん means 'half past' (くじはん = 9:30). ごぜん is a.m., ごご is p.m. A few readings change shape and are worth learning by ear: よじ (4:00), しちじ (7:00), くじ (9:00). Written out, the hour is usually the kanji 時 and the minutes 分 — tonight we read them in kana so you can hear them.",
        watchFor: "に goes on clock times and calendar points, but NOT on あした / きょう / まいにち — those never take に.",
        commonError: "Saying よんじ for four o'clock. It's よじ. Likewise しちじ and くじ, not ななじ or きゅうじ.",
        examples: [
          {
            ja: "あさ しちじに おきます。",
            reading: "あさ しちじに おきます。",
            en: "I get up at seven in the morning.",
            note: "〜じに + verb ます."
          },
          {
            ja: "じゅぎょうは くじはんに はじまります。",
            reading: "じゅぎょうは くじはんに はじまります。",
            en: "The class starts at half past nine.",
            note: "はん = half past."
          },
          {
            ja: "いま なんじですか。ごぜん じゅうじです。",
            reading: "いま なんじですか。ごぜん じゅうじです。",
            en: "What time is it now? It's ten a.m.",
            note: "ごぜん = a.m.; なんじ = what time."
          }
        ]
